; BOARDING, and every line of this is a lesson the flown route taught rather
; than a shape anyone designed up front.
;
; ONE RULE DECIDES IT ALL: land grammar means the body is OUTSIDE the water
; volume, not merely above the waterline. A body resting on a shelf under the
; surface is still swimming, so the first thing the player STANDS on has to be
; somewhere the sea is not -- either beside it, or high enough that the whole
; capsule clears its top face.
;
; THE SECOND RULE: this controller jumps GAPS, it does not climb walls. There
; is no step-up. Every rise in this level is paired with a gap for exactly
; that reason, and boarding a ship is no exception -- which is why this is a
; line of mooring posts with water between them rather than a staircase. Three
; earlier attempts (a bow step under the deck's overhang, a submerged shelf
; against the hull, a shore with steps cut into it) each failed on one of
; those two rules, and each failed on the run rather than on paper.
;
; The stack, every gap under 2.2 m and every rise under 1.2: waterline 3.3,
; shore top 1.8 with 1.5 m of water over its submerged lip so a swimmer
; settles onto it the way act 2's CoveShore and act 7's SeaShore both ask,
; post 2.9, post 3.84, deck 3.84.
[node name="BoardingShore" type="StaticBody3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 9, 0.8, -283)

